\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}
\tag{draft}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Translations and rotations form an exact plane-motor family}

\p{Retain the real degenerate frame and projective encodings of
\ref{fcap-002Q}. Put #{E=e_{01}}. Since #{E^2=0} and
#{\widetilde E=-E}, the one-axis motor

##{M_d=1-dE}

satisfies

##{M_d^{-1}=\widetilde M_d=1+dE,\qquad
M_sM_t=M_{s+t}.}

It belongs to the actual vector-generated Spin carrier because

##{M_d=e_1(e_1+de_0),\qquad
Q(e_1)=Q(e_1+de_0)=1}

\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_mem_spinGroup_of_norm_mul_norm_eq_one}.
Direct sandwich multiplication gives

##{\begin{aligned}
M_dP(w,x,y)M_d^{-1}&=P(w,x+2dw,y),\\
M_dL(d_0,a,b)M_d^{-1}&=L(d_0-2da,a,b).
\end{aligned}}

Thus the radical shear of \ref{fcap-002N} is, in the fixed projective
encoding, translation by #{(2d,0)}. The packaged nondegenerate transvection
theorem is not used.}

\p{For a general translation vector #{t=(t_x,t_y)}, define

##{T(t)=1-\frac{t_x}{2}e_{01}+\frac{t_y}{2}e_{20}.}

Each increment squares to zero, and their products in either order vanish, so

##{T(t)T(v)=T(t+v),\qquad T(t)^{-1}=T(-t).}

More precisely,

##{T(t)=
\left[e_1\left(e_1+\frac{t_x}{2}e_0\right)\right]
\left[e_2\left(e_2+\frac{t_y}{2}e_0\right)\right].}

Every displayed vector has quadratic value #{1}; hence #{T(t)} lies in the
same actual Spin carrier. This is a direct factorization in the full
degenerate Clifford algebra, not membership in a radical quotient.}

\p{Let #{c^2+s^2=1} and put

##{U=c-se_{12}=e_1(ce_1-se_2),\qquad
U^{-1}=\widetilde U=c+se_{12}.}

Again both vector factors have quadratic value #{1}. Its active spatial
matrix is

##{R_U=
\begin{pmatrix}
c^2-s^2&-2cs\\
2cs&c^2-s^2
\end{pmatrix}.}

For the ordered factorization #{M(t,U)=T(t)U}, which applies the rotation
before the translation, write #{p=(x,y)} and #{n=(a,b)}. Then

##{\begin{aligned}
(w,p)&\longmapsto (w,R_Up+wt),\\
(d,n)&\longmapsto (d-t\mathbin{\cdot}R_Un,R_Un).
\end{aligned}}

The second row is the line action. It preserves incidence because

##{(d-t\mathbin{\cdot}R_Un)w
+(R_Un)\mathbin{\cdot}(R_Up+wt)=dw+n\mathbin{\cdot}p.}

In particular, translations fix every ideal point #{(0,p)}.

The selected family is closed, with exact order and inverse

##{\begin{aligned}
M(t,U)M(v,V)&=M(t+R_Uv,UV),\\
M(t,U)^{-1}&=M(-R_U^{-1}t,U^{-1}).
\end{aligned}}

These formulas prove the claims for this family; they do not assert that
every motor is a single exponential.}

\p{Sokolov's negative-half-angle rotor and nilpotent translator fix the sign
and half-factor in this convention
\citet{(51)--(52), p. 39}{sokolov2013foundations}.
Independently, \citet{Lemma 2.2, p. 567}{dereli2010degenerate} proves
#{\exp(aF)=1+aF} for a radical vector #{F}; taking
#{F=e_0} and #{a=(t_xe_1+t_ye_2)/2} gives exactly the increment of #{T(t)}.
That paper later defines a quotient carrier
\citet{Definition 2.4, p. 570}{dereli2010degenerate}; no such quotient is
imported here. The vector-pair factorizations above establish membership in
the carrier actually used in these notes.}
